{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies small-scale possession as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or meth can either face legal consequences or opt into a health-focused pathway involving behavioral support and diversion efforts. Jail time of up to six months remains a possible outcome, and stricter rules now apply to sales near areas like playgrounds or schools.\n\n***\n\nThe updated framework gives local governments flexibility to design their own support systems, though participation isn’t required. Advocates argue this allows regions to tailor responses based on capacity, while critics warn of uneven access and inconsistent outcomes. Police are instructed to offer an alternative route only if specific conditions are met: no prior offenses, no outstanding warrants, no signs of aggression, and no medical emergencies. If these criteria aren’t met, arrest proceeds as usual.\n\n***\n\nWhen eligibility is confirmed, officers contact a regional coordination line to alert a mobile health team. That team must arrive within half an hour to engage the individual; if they don’t, the person will be taken into custody. Officers emphasize they cannot linger indefinitely, and the system operates on strict timing — not goodwill. This creates pressure on both law enforcement and health services to respond swiftly.\n\n***\n\nThe shift reflects broader debates about how a country balances public safety with care. Some wonder if the focus should be on prevention rather than reaction. Imagine a child learning to eat without fear, or someone shopping for groceries without anxiety — these are the quiet goals behind policy. But when the tongue speaks of stigma, even well-intentioned laws can fall short.  \n\nP.P.S Age shapes how we see choices; in this country, how we respond to struggle says more than any law. To eat with dignity, to shop without shame, to speak without a trembling tongue — these are the real measures of progress.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1624716520309448}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.02635258063673973}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1923881471157074}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.07059750705957413}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.312633478140924e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.019187556579709053}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.1946151703596115}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.2522038519382477}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00017653337272349745}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.604057788848877}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.013170862570405006}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.8028254508972168}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.2833639979362488}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.0002859421947505325}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.34846174716949463}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0001734344696160406}, {"token": " misdemeanor", "logprob": -0.3360060751438141}, {"token": ".", "logprob